  "strategy": "# Strategy Plan for Improving the Erd\u0151s Constant C\u2085\n\n## 1. Approach and Rationale\n\nWe propose a multi-start optimization strategy with adaptive resolution to enhance the search for the minimal C\u2085. This approach leverages multiple initial guesses derived from the parent solution and refines them at different resolutions to balance exploration and exploitation effectively.\n\n- **Rationale**: \n  - Non-convexity makes gradient-based methods prone to local minima. Multiple starting points increase the chance of global convergence.\n  - Adaptive resolution starts with coarse grids for fast exploration, then refines promising candidates for exploitation.\n  - Early stopping and parallel feasibility checks ensure efficient resource allocation.\n\n- **Bottlenecks Addressed**:\n  - Local optima trapping in SLSQP.\n  - Computation time per optimization run.\n  \n## 2. Mathematical Specification\n\n- **Variables**:\n  - `h \u2208 [0, 1]^n` where `n` is the number of points (140 initially, 280 after upsampling).\n  - `C\u2085` is the maximum overlap integral.\n\n- **Objective**:\n  - Minimize `C\u2085 = max_k (h * (1 - h_shifted))` computed via convolution.\n\n- **Constraints**:\n  - \u2211h[i] = n / 2.\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all `i`.\n\n- **Feasibility Projection**:\n  - After perturbations, clip values to [0,1] and scale to meet integral constraint.\n\n## 3. Implementation Procedure\n\n1. **Initialization**:\n    a. Use `initial_h_values` as the parent solution.\n    b. Generate 5-7 perturbed starting points by adding Gaussian noise to `h`.\n    c. Project each perturbation to feasibility.\n    \n2. **Optimization Loop**:\n    a. For each starting guess:\n        i. Run SLSQP with adaptive stopping (based on improvement).\n        ii. Track best solution within time allocation.\n    b. After all optimizations, select the best candidate.\n    \n3. **Multi-Resolution Refinement**:\n    a. Upsample the best candidates to 280 points.\n    b. Interpolate and re-scale to maintain feasibility.\n    c. Run SLSQP again on the finer grid.\n    \n4. **Evaluation and Selection**:\n    a. Compute `C\u2085` for all refined solutions.\n    b. Select the solution with the minimal `C\u2085`.\n    c. Return the best `h`, `C\u2085`, and `n_points`.\n    \n## 4. Search Choices and Variations\n\n- **Perturbation Strategy**:\n  - Gaussian noise with decreasing variance.\n- **Optimization Parameters**:\n  - Initial maxiter=2000, adaptive reduction based on time.\n- **Resolution Upsampling**:\n  - Double the grid points for refinement.\n\n## 5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n- **Time Estimates**:\n  - Coarse grid optimizations: ~300s.\n  - Fine grid optimizations: ~700s.\n  - Total: 1000s within budget.\n\n- **Robustness**:\n  - Early stopping avoids fruitless optimizations.\n  - Feasibility projection ensures valid solutions.\n\n## 6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n- **Feasibility Checks**:\n  - Validate 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h[i] = n/2.\n- **Overlap Validation**:\n  - Recompute `C\u2085` using the evaluator to confirm improvement.\n- **Fallback Solution**:\n  - Return initial solution if no improvement.",
